# Prime report — Workshop establishment, Pack B with addendum 001

Date: 2026-10-26
Reviewer: Codex, Prime
Reviewed commit: befac9fe0dacdae15522187a14a887dadad571bc, branch workshop
Builder: claude-opus-5-5
Disposition: ACCEPT the workspace-establishment result. No runtime activation,
role/law seating, lock, main merge or next-pack execution is approved by this report.

## Assessment

This is a substantial improvement over the old working environment. The desk now
exists, has one entry, and names its actual state. I could walk from README to the
assignment, goal, seats, decisions, imports, findings and bootstrap record without
reconstructing the project from previous conversations. The work is organized
around one active desk and three books. Historical alternatives are preserved and
distinguished from the small explicit selection exposed in the books.

The meaningful gain is coherent working context. Prime and Builder have a place
to put the next bounded job and its evidence. We can now improve actual executable
behavior without another broad workspace migration or kernel rewrite.

The larger Workshop application is still under construction. app/ contains a
reservation README. The loader and other runtime modules remain inactive candidate
files. There is no integrated Clerk inbox/dispatch, seating/lock implementation,
active AGENTS.md or live-model coldboot demonstration. Those omissions match this
pack's declared boundary and are not grounds to fail the establishment result.

## Independent checks

Prime wrote an independent local verifier and queried GitHub refs separately.
The following passed:
- All 1,562 tracked files match their HEAD blob bytes; the index matches HEAD.
- No gitlinks; the workspace was clean before and after verification/tests.
- All 995 import rows resolve from their declared revision:path to the pinned
  blob, length and SHA-256, and match the installed destination bytes. This covers
  890 snapshots, 81 selected copies, 22 P1 files and two Claude manifest files.
- The original 39-file baseline and new-file/final custody manifests match.
- Old checkout HEAD/status/refs/stashes/worktree registrations and captured status
  file hashes remain identical to the pre-Pack-B capture.
- GitHub workshop equals befac9fe; main, rebuild and all three archive refs remain
  at their expected commits.
- The current 00.live.md passes the renderer's check against its owning records.

Prime also reran the three bounded suites in copied labs under D:\librarian\temp,
so their tests could not rewrite the Builder's evidence: byte custody 6/6, imports
6/6, renderer 4/4. All 16 passed. Initial fixture creation was denied by sandbox
filesystem access; the isolated rerun with access succeeded. That first log is
preserved and is not counted as a product regression.

The Builder's fresh local and GitHub clone evidence was read. Prime did not make
an additional fresh remote clone; the independent checks here verify local objects
and currently published commit refs. No historical runtime candidate was executed.

## What is working well

1. **Orientation tells the truth.** It says returned, identifies the exact current
   batch, names Prime review as the dependency, and lists missing capabilities.
   It does not use green setup tests to claim the app is running.
2. **History and local authority are separated.** The selection register records
   73 reference faces and eight inactive runtime candidates. Preserved old stamps
   do not automatically become current approval. Both sides of collisions remain
   available, rather than being silently resolved by a model's preferred version.
3. **The Git storage defect produced a tested improvement.** Tracked attributes,
   index/commit checks and fresh-clone tests now address the failure found in A.
   This demonstrates a useful cycle: real failure, retained evidence, bounded
   correction, and a reusable development tool.
4. **The Builder used the stop/recovery process constructively.** The Windows
   command-line limit and self-inventory problems were exposed and corrected with
   evidence. The return records them and stops at the intended review boundary.
5. **The workflow record is becoming usable.** The bootstrap page connects packs,
   releases, batches, tools and returns. Meaningful Director replies have source
   identities. Draft role definitions distinguish the standing role from who
   executed a particular pack.

These are improvements to working conditions and recoverability. They are not a
measured productivity multiplier or proof of general multi-model autonomy.

## Findings for the next slice

### PR-B01 — Context checking currently validates syntax, not resolution

The renderer checks required keys and state vocabulary, but does not establish
that the book exists, that its workflow space is declared, or that current_work
exists inside the correct context. Prime reproduced acceptance by parse_record of
workflow_space 99, a nonexistent book, and a nonexistent work item, using in-memory
mutations only. The real record was never changed.

This is a bounded limitation of the development view. The current record is valid
and nothing executes from the view. Before adding executable dispatch, the context
resolver must reject missing/out-of-scope book, work item and undeclared space;
also test ambiguous resolution, path escape, wrong candidate hash and wrong role
or release as applicable. Do not treat a view matching its input as proof the input
names a valid or authorized job. One shared resolver should serve entry and runner
rather than duplicating the meaning in several scripts.

### PR-B02 — Prime/Builder role wording needs two targeted clarifications

0063 escalates every new design choice to Director and permits closing returns
only when there is no open question. Read literally, this can turn routine design
and nonblocking findings back into constant Director work. Preserve escalation for
changes of purpose, authority, shared contracts, budget/consequence or scope;
allow Prime to make routine choices within an explicit delegation and accept a
bounded deliverable while keeping unrelated future findings open. This is a
proposed draft clarification, not a new grant created by this report.

0065 says stop when a tool refuses. Expected negative-test refusals should instead
be evidence of a passing test. Unexpected operational refusals, unsafe conditions,
unmet dependencies and unauthorized scope still stop the affected action. Without
this distinction, the role page contradicts the very fixture gates we just used.

AGENTS.004's absolute wording about seating should likewise eventually distinguish
Director authority from compute executing an authorized seating operation. It is
currently a draft and need not be seated merely to continue released dev work.

### PR-B03 — Do not turn ordinary contextual differences into decision queues

B-F1: the same page number in another book is expected address reuse, not a same-
location collision. Record contextual identity; elevate only a real conflict in
the definition or intended shared dependency. No Director arbitration is needed
solely because two books both contain 0300.

B-F2: work function faces being versioned is conservative and caused no current
failure. Codify the exact scope with the face builder later; no rename pass now.
B-F3: the one-line desk sentinel and its pointer work for provisional entry.
Their final schema belongs in the later furniture/face contract. Neither blocks
the next fixture-based runtime slice.

Minor correction: 0041's source note still calls the handoff lab's 20 tests
"20 negative tests". They are 20 total acceptance tests. Correct when revising
that draft; the source report remains accurate. Observer was deliberately deferred
and should remain separate from this next runtime build.

### PR-B04 — Keep the next runtime pack honest about code adaptation

0401 finish line 2 pairs loader/context adaptation with a statement that code stays
unchanged. That is true for a stamp-only relocation; it cannot cover adding a
resolver, candidate-selection interface or removing old hard-coded paths. The next
pack must explicitly authorize those bounded body changes, create new versioned
candidates and preserve original/derived hashes. Do not repeat the earlier scope
contradiction by asking for new behavior while forbidding the necessary code edits.

## Recommended next action

Design one runtime slice with a visible finish line: an explicit entry can resolve
the declared book/workflow context, report where it will work, select a pinned
development candidate, and run one bounded fixture operation. Normal mode must
refuse missing active modules. No highest-version fallback, current-directory
guessing, or access to the old checkouts. Keep activation separate.

Use the relevant preserved loader/checker candidates after inspection, allow only
the named path/context/interface changes, and test every refusal against fixtures.
Add the role clarifications above to the same small design review; do not launch
another broad kernel cleanup. The F01 lint false-pass remains a tracked issue and
must be fixed before that lint can be relied on as an execution authorization gate.

After accepting this report, the assignment record should advance through the
agreed record-update operation and the view should be regenerated. This review
does not silently edit the Builder's canonical record or mark draft law approved.
There is not yet a supported transition command; the next released dev slice should
state exactly how the record is updated and by whose authority.

## Conclusion for the Director

The establishment pass did its job. We now have a coherent place to operate, a
recoverable account of what is present, and concrete targets for missing behavior.
The next gain should be one actual supported operation through compute. Another
large round of abstract kernel redesign would not be justified by this return.

Evidence is saved with this report: verification.json, the independent check
script, copied-fixture suite logs and live remote refs. No next pack was executed.
